<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Smaradahana</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Smaradahana ('Burning of the God of Love') is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-javanese">Old Javanese</a> kakawin poem written by Mpu Dharmaja at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> in the early 12th century. It tells how <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> burned Kama, the god of love, with his third eye, and how Kama and his wife were reborn in human form. It honours King Kameshvara as an incarnation of Kama.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/smaradahana.jpg" alt="Smaradahana" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: I Ketut Gedé · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smaradahana_(I_Ketut_Ged%C3%A9).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
